    <!-- Templates Section -->
    <div class="section" id="templates">
        <div class="section-header">📋 DR Templates</div>
        <div class="section-content">
            <h3>Template 1: DR Monitoring Dashboard Query</h3>
            <div class="code-block">-- Use this query in Grafana/Superset for monitoring
SELECT
    database,
    table,
    replica_name,
    absolute_delay as replication_lag_seconds,
    queue_size,
    inserts_in_queue,
    merges_in_queue,
    is_leader,
    is_readonly,
    now() as check_time
FROM system.replication_queue
WHERE database NOT IN ('system')
ORDER BY absolute_delay DESC;

-- Alert conditions:
-- - absolute_delay > 300 (5 min lag): WARN
-- - absolute_delay > 3600 (1 hour lag): CRITICAL
-- - queue_size > 1000: CRITICAL
-- - replica unavailable: CRITICAL</div>

            <h3>Template 2: Automated Backup Script</h3>
            <div class="code-block">#!/bin/bash
# Daily backup to S3 with retention policy
set -e

BACKUP_DATE=$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S)
BACKUP_DIR="/var/clickhouse/backup/$BACKUP_DATE"
S3_BUCKET="s3://my-backups/clickhouse"
RETENTION_DAYS=30

# Create backup
mkdir -p "$BACKUP_DIR"
clickhouse-client --query="BACKUP DATABASE mydb TO '$BACKUP_DIR'"

# Upload to S3
aws s3 sync "$BACKUP_DIR" "$S3_BUCKET/$BACKUP_DATE"

# Clean old backups (locally keep last 3)
find /var/clickhouse/backup -maxdepth 1 -type d -mtime +3 -exec rm -rf {} \;

# Log backup status
echo "Backup $BACKUP_DATE completed successfully" >> /var/log/backups.log

# Send notification
curl -X POST https://alerts.example.com/backup \
  -d "status=success&date=$BACKUP_DATE&size=$(du -sh $BACKUP_DIR | cut -f1)"</div>

            <h3>Template 3: Multi-Datacenter Config</h3>
            <div class="code-block"># /etc/clickhouse-server/config.d/remote_servers.xml
&lt;yandex&gt;
    &lt;remote_servers&gt;
        &lt;mycluster&gt;
            &lt;shard&gt;
                &lt;!-- Replica 1 in DC1 --&gt;
                &lt;replica&gt;
                    &lt;host&gt;ch1.dc1.example.com&lt;/host&gt;
                    &lt;port&gt;9000&lt;/port&gt;
                &lt;/replica&gt;
                &lt;!-- Replica 2 in DC1 --&gt;
                &lt;replica&gt;
                    &lt;host&gt;ch2.dc1.example.com&lt;/host&gt;
                    &lt;port&gt;9000&lt;/port&gt;
                &lt;/replica&gt;
            &lt;/shard&gt;
            &lt;shard&gt;
                &lt;!-- Replica 1 in DC2 --&gt;
                &lt;replica&gt;
                    &lt;host&gt;ch1.dc2.example.com&lt;/host&gt;
                    &lt;port&gt;9000&lt;/port&gt;
                &lt;/replica&gt;
            &lt;/shard&gt;
        &lt;/mycluster&gt;
    &lt;/remote_servers&gt;
&lt;/yandex&gt;</div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Advanced Patterns Section -->
    <div class="section" id="advanced">
        <div class="section-header">🔥 Advanced DR Patterns</div>
        <div class="section-content">
            <h3>1. Cascading Replication (Multi-Tier)</h3>
            <p>For very large clusters, use cascading replication to reduce network traffic</p>
            <div class="code-block">PRIMARY CLUSTER       REGIONAL REPLICAS      EDGE REPLICAS
(US-East)             (US-West, EU, APAC)    (City-level)

DC1-Primary ────────→ DC2 Replica ────────→ DC3 Replica (read-only)
    │                     │
    │                     └───────→ DC4 Replica (Europe) ──→ DC5 Replica (read-only)
    │
    └────────→ DC6 Replica (APAC) ──→ DC7 Replica (read-only)

Benefits:
- Primary only replicates to 3 regional replicas
- Regional replicas propagate to edge locations
- Reduces network load on primary
- Better latency for edge queries</div>

            <h3>2. Hybrid Active-Active with Regional Partitioning</h3>
            <div class="code-block">-- DC1 handles US region writes
CREATE TABLE events (
    event_time DateTime,
    region LowCardinality(String),
    data String
) ENGINE = ReplicatedMergeTree(...)
PARTITION BY (region, toYYYYMM(event_time))
ORDER BY (region, event_time)
WHERE region IN ('US-East', 'US-West');

-- DC2 handles EU region writes
-- DC3 handles APAC region writes

-- Global view (for queries)
CREATE VIEW events_global AS
SELECT * FROM events
UNION ALL
SELECT * FROM remote('dc2', 'db', 'events')
UNION ALL
SELECT * FROM remote('dc3', 'db', 'events');

Benefits:
- Each region primary handles its writes
- No write conflicts (partitioned by region)
- Can failover region independently
- Near-local latency for reads/writes</div>

            <h3>3. Automatic Failover with Health Checks</h3>
            <div class="code-block">-- Monitoring script that detects failures
#!/bin/bash
PRIMARY="ch-primary.example.com:8123"
SECONDARY="ch-secondary.example.com:8123"
CHECK_INTERVAL=10
FAILURE_THRESHOLD=3

consecutive_failures=0

while true; do
    # Check primary health
    if ! curl -s "http://$PRIMARY/?query=SELECT%201" | grep -q "1"; then
        ((consecutive_failures++))
        if [ $consecutive_failures -ge $FAILURE_THRESHOLD ]; then
            # Trigger failover
            echo "Primary failed $FAILURE_THRESHOLD times. Initiating failover..."

            # Verify secondary is healthy
            if curl -s "http://$SECONDARY/?query=SELECT%20COUNT(*)" > /dev/null; then
                # Update DNS
                aws route53 change-resource-record-sets \
                  --hosted-zone-id ZONE_ID \
                  --change-batch "{ \"Changes\": [{ \"Action\": \"UPSERT\", \"ResourceRecordSet\": { \"Name\": \"clickhouse.example.com\", \"Type\": \"A\", \"TTL\": 30, \"ResourceRecords\": [{\"Value\": \"$SECONDARY\"}] } }] }"

                # Notify team
                slack_notify "Failover completed: traffic now to secondary"
                consecutive_failures=0
            fi
        fi
    else
        consecutive_failures=0
    fi
    sleep $CHECK_INTERVAL
done</div>

            <h3>4. ZooKeeper Cluster Setup for DR</h3>
            <div class="code-block">-- Optimal ZooKeeper setup for disaster recovery
-- Use 5 nodes total:
-- - 3 in primary datacenter (voting members)
-- - 2 in secondary datacenter (voting members or observers)

# zoo.cfg on all servers
server.1=zk1.dc1.example.com:2888:3888
server.2=zk2.dc1.example.com:2888:3888
server.3=zk3.dc1.example.com:2888:3888
server.4=zk1.dc2.example.com:2888:3888
server.5=zk2.dc2.example.com:2888:3888

# For DC2 observers (non-voting):
server.4=zk1.dc2.example.com:2888:3888:observer
server.5=zk2.dc2.example.com:2888:3888:observer

-- Ensures quorum survives primary datacenter failure
-- Voting members: 3 (majority of 5)
-- Can lose primary DC and still have quorum</div>
        </div>
    </div>

